issue='The source requests a combinatorial characterization but does not specify a candidate higher-arity decomposition, parameter or obstruction condition whose equivalence is to be proved. The transduction-based property itself is recoverable and is defined below; merely restating it is not the requested new characterization. The graph low-shrubdepth-cover theorem cannot silently be declared a conjecture about an undefined relational analogue. A precise proposed characterization, or a specified class of admissible structural answers, is still needed to turn this broad research request into an unambiguous benchmark target. The question type remains provisional until that target is fixed.'
notes=['Recovered finite fixed relational signature and output-side higher arity, rather than changing the source structures alone.','Defined bounded-expansion source classes and fixed finite-copy unary-parameter first-order transductions.','Separated the proved graph cover theorem and 2023 graph decomposition characterizations from the relational target.','Retained the source’s broad characterization request and marked the missing answer predicate instead of inventing a relational shrubdepth conjecture.']
save(i,dict(title='Structural characterization of bounded-expansion transductions',formal='For a fixed finite relational signature Σ, characterize the classes C of finite Σ-structures for which there exist a bounded-expansion graph class D and a fixed first-order transduction T with C⊆T(D). The source asks for a combinatorial characterization when output relations may have arity greater than two, extending its completed graph-class characterization. The existential transduction property is defined below, but the requested new structural condition is not specified in the source, so this card is not yet a complete proposition with a fixed acceptance criterion.',definitions='A finite relational signature Σ lists finitely many relation symbols, each with a fixed finite arity. A finite Σ-structure A has a finite universe V(A) and an interpretation Rᴬ⊆V(A)^r for each r-ary symbol R; relations need not be symmetric, irreflexive or binary. The signature is fixed across the class, independently of structure size. First-order formulas use relation symbols, equality, Boolean connectives and quantifiers over individual elements; they do not quantify over sets or relations. A source graph is finite, simple and undirected. An r-shallow minor is formed by selecting pairwise vertex-disjoint connected branch subgraphs, each of radius at most r measured within that subgraph, contracting each to one vertex, and deleting vertices or edges; loops are removed and parallel edges merged. A graph class D has bounded expansion if for every nonnegative integer r there is a finite bound f(r) such that every nonempty r-shallow minor H of every G∈D has 2|E(H)|/|V(H)|≤f(r). No particular growth rate or effective representation of f is stipulated. A transduction uses a fixed finite sequence of the following operations: introduce unary predicates with arbitrarily chosen interpretations; make a fixed finite number of copies of the current universe, retaining the relations in each copy and a relation identifying copies of the same element; define new relations by fixed first-order formulas; restrict the universe by a fixed unary formula; and discard symbols. The source also allows intermediate partial unary functions defined by unique witnesses, which can be represented by their binary graphs. All formulas, numbers of copies and numbers of unary parameters are fixed for T, independently of the input graph. Output elements are retained copied vertices, not arbitrary tuples of input vertices. T(G) is the set of outputs obtained over unary-parameter choices, and T(D) is their union over G∈D, considered up to isomorphism. The quantifiers are ∃D,T ∀A∈C ∃G∈D and parameter choices producing A; no single input structure may receive a separately chosen formula of unbounded length. This is a property of whole classes, with no promise that C is supplied by a finite algorithm. For context, a graph class has bounded shrubdepth if there are constants d,m such that each graph admits a rooted tree with leaves exactly its vertices, all leaves at depth d and colored with m colors, where leaf adjacency is determined by their two colors and their tree distance. It has low shrubdepth covers if for every p≥1 there are constants N,d,m such that every graph in the class has a cover by at most N vertex sets, every set of at most p vertices lies inside one cover member, and each induced cover graph has such a tree model with bounds d,m. The constants may depend on p and the class, not graph size. This definition and the cited equivalence concern graphs; they do not define a decomposition for arbitrary Σ-relations.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='The intended result is a necessary-and-sufficient combinatorial condition for exactly the classes C with the transduction property above, uniformly for fixed finite relational signatures. The proposed condition and its full quantifiers must first be made explicit, then both directions established. A graph-only theorem, changing only the source signature, or renaming the defining transduction property does not supply the requested characterization. No particular relational cover condition is currently certified as the source’s conjecture.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Dense structure inherited from sparse graphs',text='A first-order transduction can produce dense outputs from uniformly sparse inputs. The graph theorem characterizes such outputs through low shrubdepth covers, giving structural information without requiring a supplied sparse preimage.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The unresolved extension concerns output relations',text='The source notes that allowing bounded-expansion relational structures on the input side does not change the graph-output classes. Its next question instead asks about higher-arity output structures. Passing to a Gaifman graph records which elements co-occur in tuples, but loses the tuples’ relation symbols, positions and other information.',citation='full'),dict(title='Later characterizations still have graph outputs',text='Dreier’s 2023 Theorem 1.6 gives equivalent lacon-, shrub- and parity-decomposition characterizations of structurally bounded expansion. The theorem explicitly quantifies over graph classes, so it is not itself the general relational characterization requested here.',citation='lacon'),dict(title='Structural and algorithmic questions differ',text='The source connects structural descriptions with first-order model checking, but obtaining an algorithm also requires access to suitable representations or decompositions. An efficient model-checking theorem alone does not specify the missing higher-arity characterization.',citation='primary')],why='A structural description for higher-arity relations would extend the theory connecting sparse representations, dense structures and logical tractability beyond ordinary graphs. It must retain information in entire tuples, which cannot be recovered from an adjacency graph alone.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2018',text='The graph low-shrubdepth-cover characterization is proved; the source explicitly leaves higher-arity relational output classes for further characterization.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2020-07-05',text='The graph characterization appears in the journal version; publisher abstract and publication history checked.',citation='journal'),dict(date='2023-06-06',text='LMCS publication gives further equivalent decompositions for graph classes of structurally bounded expansion.',citation='lacon')],status='uncertain',status_note='Read the original and full 2018 models and conclusion, checked the 2020 journal abstract and publication history, and read the 2023 decomposition theorem and its graph-only transduction model. Searched subsequent work without finding a matching unrestricted relational-output characterization. The original target remains a broad characterization request with an unspecified structural answer condition. Journal full text was inaccessible; no exhaustive status survey or independent proof verification is claimed.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(status='needs_specification',reviewed_on=DATE,notes=notes,remaining_issue=issue),review_note=issue,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='Extends structural sparsity beyond binary graph relations and could support general finite-model-theoretic applications; a precise structural acceptance condition remains necessary. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['First-order transductions can encode dense structures using sparse graph sources.','For graph outputs, low shrubdepth covers give a known combinatorial characterization.','The source asks for a corresponding characterization when outputs have relations of higher arity.','The 2023 alternative decomposition theorem still concerns graphs and does not by itself answer that extension.','The transduction property is now defined, but the requested new structural answer condition remains to be specified.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','full','journal','lacon'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs],state='improved_unfinished')
